1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Here's how the Arduino decides which LEDs to turn on in each
mode.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:20,000
The ones LED is on the far right. Moving left, the values are
two, four, eight, and sixteen. The code starts at the
rightmost LED.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:33,000
To display thirteen, the one, four, and eight lights are on.
One plus four plus eight equals thirteen. The two and sixteen
lights stay off.

4
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:46,000
Each short press and release calls incrementCounter, which
adds one. Then showBinary reads the five bits of that number
and updates the lights.

5
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:59,000
bitRead shifts the number right, then uses bitwise AND with
one. The shift moves the chosen bit to the rightmost position.
AND keeps only that bit.

6
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:07,000
Shifting thirteen by zero leaves its bits unchanged. AND with
one returns one, so pin twelve turns on.

7
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Thirteen shifted right once ends in zero. AND with one gives
zero, so the twos LED is off.

8
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Shift thirteen right twice. The last bit is one. AND with one
keeps it, turning on the fours LED.

9
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:31,000
Shift thirteen right three times. The result is one. AND with
one keeps it, turning on the eights LED.

10
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Shift thirteen right four times. The result is zero. AND with
one keeps zero, so the sixteens LED stays off.

11
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:50,000
The one, four, and eight LEDs are on for thirteen. At
thirty-one, all five are on. Another press resets the count to
zero.

12
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:02,000
Logical operators work with true-or-false answers. runProjectB
reads the two buttons. A pressed button becomes true; a
released button becomes false.

13
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:13,000
NOT A asks, is A released? With A released, NOT A is true and
its LED is on. Pressing A makes it false.

14
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:24,000
AND asks, are both buttons pressed? Its LED turns on only when
A and B are both true.

15
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:35,000
OR asks, is at least one button pressed? A alone, B alone, or
both together make OR true.

16
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:48,000
Exclusive OR asks, is exactly one button pressed? A alone or B
alone makes it true. Press both, and exclusive OR turns off.

17
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:55,000
With neither button pressed, the only output light on is NOT
A.

18
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:02,000
With A alone, the A indicator, OR, and exclusive OR are on.

19
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:09,000
With B alone, B, NOT A, OR, and exclusive OR are on.

20
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:18,000
With both pressed, A, B, AND, and OR are on. Exclusive OR is
off.

21
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:30,000
For every result, digitalWrite turns the matching LED on for
true and off for false. The main loop repeats, so the display
responds whenever the buttons change.
